159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 91 0 R
/E (To improve the security of the login process, the system was updated to use password hashing instead of comparing the password as plain text. A new class named PasswordHasher was added to generate a SHA-256 hash combined with a unique salt value. The users table in the database was also modified by adding two new columns: password_hash and salt.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
160 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 91 0 R
/E (During login, the system retrieves the stored password hash and salt for the entered username. Then, it hashes the entered password using the same salt and compares the generated hash with the stored hash. If both values match, the login is accepted. This approach improves password protection because the original password is not directly stored or compared in the database.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
161 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 91 0 R
/T (Table: Password Hashing Implementation)
/E (Table: Password Hashing Implementation)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
162 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 91 0 R
/E (This implementation adds an additional security layer to the authentication process. Even if the database is accessed, the actual password cannot be directly read because only the hashed value and salt are stored.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
